/* Interview bank: model answers written for spoken delivery (lead with the answer, then the mechanism, then the trade-off). */
MM.INTERVIEW.categories = [
{
  id: 'developer',
  title: 'Developer',
  desc: 'CRUD, query operators, aggregation, schema modelling and indexes from the application side.',
  items: [
  { level: 'junior', tags: ['basics'], q: 'What is MongoDB and when would you not choose it?',
    a: `<p>MongoDB is a distributed document database: data is stored as BSON documents in collections, with a flexible schema, secondary indexes, an aggregation framework, multi-document ACID transactions, built-in replication (replica sets) and horizontal scaling (sharding).</p>
<p>I would not pick it when the workload is dominated by many-to-many relationships queried from every direction with ad-hoc joins, when the team needs heavy cross-entity reporting with strict relational constraints, or when the data is tiny and an existing relational database already does the job. The document model pays off when the unit you read and write is naturally an aggregate (an order with its lines, a user profile with settings).</p>` },
  { level: 'junior', tags: ['bson'], q: 'What is BSON and why does MongoDB not just store JSON?',
    a: `<p>BSON is a binary, length-prefixed serialization of documents. It adds types JSON does not have (ObjectId, Date, Decimal128, Int32/Int64, Double, Binary, Timestamp, Regex) and length prefixes so the server can skip over fields without parsing them.</p>
<p>Practical consequences: dates should be stored as Date, not strings, so range queries and date operators work; money should be Decimal128 or integer minor units, not Double; and a document is limited to 16 MB of BSON.</p>` },
  { level: 'junior', tags: ['objectid'], q: 'What is inside an ObjectId and can you rely on it for ordering?',
    a: `<p>12 bytes: a 4-byte seconds-since-epoch timestamp, a 5-byte random value unique to the process, and a 3-byte incrementing counter. It is generated by the driver, so no server round trip is needed.</p>
<p>Ordering is only roughly by creation time: one-second resolution, and clocks on different app servers can differ. Use it for "approximately newest first", not as a strict sequence. Because it is monotonically increasing, it is a poor <em>ranged</em> shard key (all inserts hit the last chunk).</p>` },
  { level: 'junior', tags: ['crud'], q: 'What is the difference between updateOne with a replacement document and with update operators?',
    a: `<p><code>updateOne(filter, { $set: {...} })</code> modifies the named fields only. <code>replaceOne(filter, doc)</code> replaces the whole document except <code>_id</code>. Modern drivers reject <code>updateOne</code> with a plain document (no operators) precisely to stop accidental whole-document replacement.</p>
<p>Operators are also better for concurrency: <code>$inc</code>, <code>$push</code> and <code>$set</code> on a subset of fields are atomic on the server, so two clients incrementing a counter never lose an update, whereas read-modify-replace in the application does.</p>` },
  { level: 'junior', tags: ['crud', 'upsert'], q: 'How does an upsert work, and what is the race condition people hit?',
    a: `<p>With <code>{ upsert: true }</code>, if no document matches the filter, MongoDB inserts one built from the equality fields of the filter plus the update (and <code>$setOnInsert</code> fields only on insert).</p>
<p>The race: two concurrent upserts with the same filter can both find nothing and both insert, creating duplicates. The fix is a unique index on the filter fields; then one insert wins and the other gets a duplicate key error that the server or driver retries as an update.</p>` },
  { level: 'junior', tags: ['query'], q: 'Explain the difference between { tags: "a" } and { tags: ["a"] } on an array field.',
    a: `<p><code>{ tags: "a" }</code> matches any document whose tags array <em>contains</em> "a". <code>{ tags: ["a"] }</code> matches only documents whose tags array is <em>exactly</em> <code>["a"]</code> (same elements, same order). For "contains all of", use <code>$all</code>; for "contains any of", use <code>$in</code>.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['query', 'arrays'], q: 'When do you need $elemMatch?',
    a: `<p>When several conditions must hold for the <em>same</em> array element. <code>{ "items.category": "electronics", "items.qty": { $gte: 2 } }</code> can be satisfied by two different line items. <code>{ items: { $elemMatch: { category: "electronics", qty: { $gte: 2 } } } }</code> requires one line item to satisfy both.</p>
<p>The same trap applies to range conditions on scalar arrays: <code>{ scores: { $gt: 80, $lt: 85 } }</code> matches <code>[90, 10]</code> because one element satisfies each bound.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['pagination'], q: 'Why is skip/limit pagination slow on deep pages, and what do you use instead?',
    a: `<p><code>skip(n)</code> still walks n index keys (or documents) and throws them away, so page 5,000 costs 5,000 pages of work. It is also unstable when documents are inserted between requests.</p>
<p>Use keyset (range) pagination: sort on a unique, indexed key such as <code>{ createdAt: -1, _id: -1 }</code> and ask for the next page with <code>{ $or: [{ createdAt: { $lt: lastTs } }, { createdAt: lastTs, _id: { $lt: lastId } }] }</code>. Each page is then an index seek plus a short scan.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['aggregation'], q: 'Walk me through how an aggregation pipeline executes and how you keep it fast.',
    a: `<p>Documents stream through stages in order; each stage's output is the next stage's input. The optimizer reorders and coalesces where it is safe (moves <code>$match</code> before <code>$project</code>, merges <code>$sort</code> + <code>$limit</code> into a top-k sort, pushes <code>$match</code> into the query layer).</p>
<p>To keep it fast: put a selective <code>$match</code> first so it can use an index; sort on indexed fields before any stage that changes document shape; project away large fields early; remember that blocking stages (<code>$group</code>, <code>$sort</code> without index) hold data in memory up to 100 MB per stage before needing <code>allowDiskUse</code> (on by default since 6.0); and make <code>$lookup</code> targets indexed on the foreign field.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['aggregation', 'lookup'], q: 'How does $lookup perform and when is it the wrong tool?',
    a: `<p><code>$lookup</code> is a left outer join executed per input document: for each document it queries the foreign collection. With an index on the foreign field each probe is an index seek; without one, each probe is a collection scan, which is quadratic.</p>
<p>It is the wrong tool on the hot read path when the joined data is small and read together every time: embed or extend-reference it instead. It is fine for reporting, back-office screens and occasional joins. Since 5.1 it can target a sharded collection; before that the foreign collection had to be unsharded.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['schema'], q: 'Embed or reference? Give me your decision rule.',
    a: `<p>Embed when the child data is owned by the parent, read together with it, bounded in size, and rarely updated independently (order lines, addresses). Reference when the child is shared across many parents, grows without bound, is large, or is queried on its own (products referenced from orders, comments on a viral post).</p>
<p>Middle ground: the extended reference pattern copies the few fields you display (product name and price at purchase) into the parent, which also captures historical values. The real rule: model for the queries you run most, and keep documents well under 16 MB with arrays that cannot grow forever.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['schema', 'patterns'], q: 'Name four schema design patterns and the problem each one solves.',
    a: `<ul><li><b>Bucket</b>: group many small measurements per document (per device per hour) to cut index size and document count. Time-series collections automate this.</li>
<li><b>Computed</b>: keep a precomputed total or counter updated on write so reads do not aggregate every time.</li>
<li><b>Subset</b>: embed only the most recent N reviews and keep the rest in a separate collection, so the hot document stays small.</li>
<li><b>Attribute</b>: turn many sparse fields into <code>[{ k, v }]</code> so one compound index on <code>{ "attrs.k": 1, "attrs.v": 1 }</code> covers them all.</li></ul>
<p>Others worth naming: outlier (overflow documents for the rare huge case), extended reference, schema versioning, polymorphic, tree (parent/ancestors).</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['indexes'], q: 'What is a compound index and what is the ESR rule?',
    a: `<p>A compound index sorts keys by the first field, then by the second within ties, and so on. A query can use any <em>prefix</em> of it.</p>
<p>ESR orders the fields: <b>Equality</b> fields first, then <b>Sort</b> fields, then <b>Range</b> fields. Equality first narrows the scan to one contiguous slice; sort next means that slice is already in the requested order so there is no in-memory SORT stage; range last bounds the scan without breaking the sort order. Putting the range before the sort forces a blocking sort over every key in the range.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['indexes'], q: 'What is a covered query and how do you prove one?',
    a: `<p>A query is covered when the filter and the projection use only fields in one index, so the server answers from index keys without fetching documents. You must exclude <code>_id</code> unless it is in the index.</p>
<p>Proof: <code>explain("executionStats")</code> shows a PROJECTION_COVERED stage over IXSCAN with no FETCH, and <code>totalDocsExamined: 0</code>. Multikey indexes cannot cover queries on the array field.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['explain'], q: 'Which explain() fields do you look at first?',
    a: `<p>From <code>executionStats</code>: <code>nReturned</code>, <code>totalKeysExamined</code>, <code>totalDocsExamined</code> and <code>executionTimeMillis</code>; from the plan: the winning stage tree (COLLSCAN vs IXSCAN, presence of a SORT stage, FETCH with a filter).</p>
<p>The key ratio is examined-to-returned. Ideal is close to 1:1 for keys and docs. Many keys per result means a poorly ordered or unselective index; many docs per result with IXSCAN means the index does not include the filtering fields; a SORT stage means the sort is not served by the index.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['indexes', 'multikey'], q: 'What is a multikey index and what are its limits?',
    a: `<p>When an indexed field holds an array, MongoDB creates one index key per element and marks the index multikey. It makes array membership queries fast.</p>
<p>Limits: a compound index can have at most one array field per document (otherwise "cannot index parallel arrays"); multikey indexes cannot cover queries on the array field; bounds on the array field may not be intersected, so range queries can scan more; and documents with large arrays create many index keys, inflating index size and write cost.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['indexes'], q: 'Partial index versus sparse index?',
    a: `<p>A sparse index skips documents that do not have the field. A partial index skips documents that do not match an arbitrary <code>partialFilterExpression</code> (for example <code>{ status: "active" }</code> or <code>{ coupon: { $exists: true } }</code>). Partial is the general, preferred form.</p>
<p>Gotcha: the planner uses a partial index only when the query filter implies the partial filter. Combined with <code>unique</code>, a partial index enforces uniqueness only among matching documents, which is how you do "unique if present".</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['transactions'], q: 'When would you use a multi-document transaction and what does it cost?',
    a: `<p>When an invariant spans documents that cannot be co-located: moving money between two accounts, decrementing stock and creating an order atomically. Single-document writes are already atomic, so model to avoid transactions where you can.</p>
<p>Cost: requires a replica set or sharded cluster; holds WiredTiger snapshots and locks until commit; default 60-second lifetime (<code>transactionLifetimeLimitSeconds</code>); write conflicts abort with <code>TransientTransactionError</code>, so the application must retry the whole transaction (the driver <code>withTransaction</code> helper does this); and cross-shard transactions add two-phase commit latency.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['transactions', 'consistency'], q: 'What is causal consistency and how do you get read-your-own-writes on secondaries?',
    a: `<p>A causally consistent session guarantees that reads observe the session's previous writes and that operations happen in an order that respects causality, even when reads go to secondaries.</p>
<p>Mechanism: the session carries <code>operationTime</code> and <code>clusterTime</code>; a read with <code>readConcern: "majority"</code> and <code>afterClusterTime</code> waits on the secondary until it has applied up to that point. You need writes with <code>w: "majority"</code> and reads with <code>"majority"</code> read concern for the guarantee to hold across failovers.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['change streams'], q: 'How do change streams work and how do you make a consumer resumable?',
    a: `<p>Change streams are a cursor over the oplog, exposed through <code>watch()</code> on a collection, database or deployment, filtered with an aggregation pipeline. Events are only returned once majority-committed, so they never roll back.</p>
<p>Each event has a resume token in <code>_id</code>. Persist the token after processing each event (ideally in the same transaction as the side effect, or idempotently), and restart with <code>resumeAfter</code> or <code>startAfter</code>. Resuming only works while the token's position is still in the oplog, so the oplog window must exceed your worst-case consumer outage. For deletes you get only the <code>_id</code> unless pre-images are enabled.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['schema validation'], q: 'How would you introduce schema validation on a busy collection with dirty legacy data?',
    a: `<p>Write a <code>$jsonSchema</code> validator, then roll out with <code>collMod</code> using <code>validationAction: "warn"</code> first, so violations are logged but writes succeed. Find existing violators with <code>db.coll.find({ $nor: [{ $jsonSchema: schema }] })</code>, fix or migrate them, then switch to <code>validationAction: "error"</code>.</p>
<p><code>validationLevel: "moderate"</code> applies rules only to inserts and to updates of documents that already pass, which lets legacy documents live until they are touched. Version your schema with a <code>schemaVersion</code> field so the application can read both shapes during migration.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['performance', 'driver'], q: 'An endpoint does 40 small queries per request. How do you redesign it?',
    a: `<p>That is the N+1 pattern. Options in order of preference: change the document model so one read returns the aggregate (embed or extended reference); batch the lookups into one <code>$in</code> query and join in memory; or use a single aggregation with <code>$lookup</code> on an indexed foreign field.</p>
<p>Then verify with the profiler or <code>$currentOp</code> that the per-request operation count dropped, and that each remaining query is index-backed with a sane examined-to-returned ratio.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['text', 'search'], q: 'Classic $text index versus Atlas Search / mongot-style search?',
    a: `<p>A <code>$text</code> index is a built-in inverted index with stemming and stop words, one per collection, scored with a simple TF-style weight. It is fine for basic keyword search, with no fuzzy matching, autocomplete, facets or rich relevance tuning.</p>
<p>Lucene-based search (<code>$search</code>, and <code>$vectorSearch</code> for embeddings) runs in a separate process (mongot) kept in sync from the oplog, so it is eventually consistent. It supports analyzers, fuzzy matching, synonyms, facets and hybrid lexical-plus-vector ranking. For self-managed deployments it is available in recent Community and Enterprise releases. Choose based on the relevance requirements, not habit.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['ai', 'vector'], q: 'Design retrieval for a RAG chatbot on top of MongoDB.',
    a: `<p>Chunk the source documents (a few hundred tokens with overlap), embed each chunk, and store <code>{ text, embedding, source, tenantId, updatedAt }</code> in one collection. Create a vector search index on <code>embedding</code> with <code>tenantId</code> declared as a filter field.</p>
<p>At query time, embed the question and run <code>$vectorSearch</code> with <code>numCandidates</code> around 10 to 20 times <code>limit</code> and a pre-filter on tenant, then <code>$project</code> the score. Optionally combine with lexical search through rank fusion. Keep embeddings in sync on update (a change stream worker re-embeds changed chunks), and evaluate retrieval quality with a fixed question set before tuning prompts.</p>` }
  ]
},
{
  id: 'dba',
  title: 'DBA / Operations',
  desc: 'Replica sets, oplog, backup and restore, security, monitoring and upgrades on self-managed MongoDB.',
  items: [
  { level: 'junior', tags: ['replication'], q: 'Describe a replica set and the role of each member type.',
    a: `<p>A replica set is a group of mongod processes holding the same data: one primary takes all writes and records them in its oplog; secondaries replicate the oplog and apply it; an arbiter votes in elections but holds no data.</p>
<p>Variants: priority 0 members can never become primary (for example a DR copy); hidden members are priority 0 and invisible to clients (backups, analytics); delayed members apply the oplog with a lag to protect against human error. At most 7 voting members, up to 50 total.</p>` },
  { level: 'junior', tags: ['replication'], q: 'Why should a replica set have an odd number of voting members?',
    a: `<p>An election needs a strict majority of voting members. With 4 voters you still need 3, so you tolerate the same single failure as with 3 voters while paying for an extra node, and a 2-2 network split leaves both halves without a primary. An odd count maximizes fault tolerance per node. Across data centers, place members so no single site holds a majority if you want to survive a site loss automatically.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['elections'], q: 'What happens step by step when the primary dies?',
    a: `<ol><li>Secondaries stop receiving heartbeats; after <code>electionTimeoutMillis</code> (10 s default) an eligible secondary calls an election and increments the term.</li>
<li>Voters grant a vote if the candidate's oplog is at least as up to date as theirs and they have not voted in this term. A majority of votes makes it primary.</li>
<li>The new primary drains its remaining oplog, then accepts writes. Drivers rediscover topology and retryable writes are retried once.</li>
<li>When the old primary returns, any writes it accepted that were not majority-replicated are rolled back into a <code>rollback/</code> directory.</li></ol>
<p>Typical failover is a few seconds to about 12 seconds end to end. <code>w: "majority"</code> writes are never rolled back.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['oplog'], q: 'What is the oplog and how do you size it?',
    a: `<p>The oplog (<code>local.oplog.rs</code>) is a capped collection of idempotent operations that secondaries tail and apply. Its time window is how long a secondary can be offline (or a backup can lag) and still catch up without a full initial sync.</p>
<p>Size it from write rate, not disk size: measure GB of oplog per hour at peak (<code>rs.printReplicationInfo()</code> shows the current window) and size for your longest planned maintenance plus margin, typically 24 to 72 hours at peak. Resize online with <code>replSetResizeOplog</code>, and set <code>minRetentionHours</code> to guarantee a time window.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['replication lag'], q: 'A secondary is lagging. How do you diagnose it?',
    a: `<p>Confirm with <code>rs.printSecondaryReplicationInfo()</code> or <code>replSetGetStatus</code> (compare <code>optimeDate</code>). Then check the usual causes in order:</p>
<ul><li>Resource saturation on the secondary: disk IOPS and latency, CPU, cache eviction pressure (it applies the same writes as the primary plus any reads you send to it).</li>
<li>Network bandwidth or latency to the sync source; check which member it is syncing from.</li>
<li>Bulk operations on the primary such as huge <code>updateMany</code>, index builds, or <code>deleteMany</code> without batching.</li>
<li>Long-running reads on the secondary holding back the applier, and missing indexes for updates (each replicated update by non-_id filter has already been converted to _id, but secondary indexes still cost).</li></ul>
<p>If lag exceeds the oplog window the member goes stale and needs a resync.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['write concern'], q: 'Explain w:1, w:"majority" and j:true.',
    a: `<p><code>w: 1</code> acknowledges once the primary applied the write in memory; it can be rolled back if the primary fails before replicating. <code>w: "majority"</code> waits until a majority of voting, data-bearing members have it, so it survives any failover. <code>j: true</code> additionally requires the journal to be flushed on disk on the acknowledging members.</p>
<p>Since 5.0 the implicit default is <code>w: "majority"</code> (except with arbiters in some topologies). <code>wtimeout</code> bounds the wait but does not undo the write.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['read concern'], q: 'What do readConcern local, majority, linearizable and snapshot guarantee?',
    a: `<ul><li><b>local</b>: latest data on the node, may be rolled back.</li>
<li><b>majority</b>: only data acknowledged by a majority, never rolled back; served from a majority-committed snapshot, so it does not block.</li>
<li><b>linearizable</b>: primary only, single document; reflects all majority writes that completed before the read started. Slow; use with <code>maxTimeMS</code>.</li>
<li><b>snapshot</b>: a consistent point-in-time view across documents, used in transactions and with <code>atClusterTime</code>.</li></ul>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['backup'], q: 'Compare mongodump, filesystem snapshots and continuous backup.',
    a: `<p><b>mongodump/mongorestore</b>: logical, portable, slow on large data, indexes are rebuilt on restore; use <code>--oplog</code> on a replica set for a consistent dump. Fine for small datasets and migrations.</p>
<p><b>Filesystem or volume snapshots</b>: fast and consistent if the journal is on the same volume (or after <code>fsyncLock</code>); restore is the size of a copy. Sharded clusters need the balancer stopped and coordinated snapshots.</p>
<p><b>Continuous backup with oplog</b> (Ops Manager, Percona Backup for MongoDB, operator-based PITR): periodic base snapshot plus oplog slices, giving point-in-time restore. This is what production needs for an RPO in seconds.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['backup', 'pitr'], q: 'Someone dropped a collection at 14:05. Walk me through point-in-time recovery.',
    a: `<ol><li>Find the exact drop timestamp in the oplog: <code>use local; db.oplog.rs.find({ op: "c", "o.drop": "orders" })</code>.</li>
<li>Restore the latest base backup taken before 14:05 to a separate, isolated instance (never straight over production).</li>
<li>Replay the oplog from the backup's point up to just before the drop: <code>mongorestore --oplogReplay --oplogLimit &lt;ts&gt;</code>, or the PBM / Ops Manager PITR target time.</li>
<li>Validate counts and spot-check documents, then copy the collection back to production (dump/restore of that one namespace) and reconcile writes made after 14:05 if needed.</li></ol>
<p>Afterwards: tighten roles so application users cannot drop collections, and test restores regularly; an untested backup is a hope, not a backup.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['security'], q: 'What is your baseline security checklist for a self-managed cluster?',
    a: `<ul><li>Enable authentication (SCRAM-SHA-256 or x.509/LDAP/OIDC) and keyfile or x.509 internal auth between members.</li>
<li>Least-privilege roles: application users get <code>readWrite</code> on their database or a custom role, never <code>root</code>; separate admin, backup and monitoring users.</li>
<li>TLS for client and intra-cluster traffic; <code>net.bindIp</code> restricted to private interfaces; firewall to app subnets only.</li>
<li>Encryption at rest (Enterprise WiredTiger encryption or Percona's equivalent, or volume encryption), and CSFLE / Queryable Encryption for the most sensitive fields.</li>
<li>Auditing enabled where available; patch cadence; disable server-side JavaScript if unused.</li></ul>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['monitoring'], q: 'Which metrics do you alert on for a production replica set?',
    a: `<ul><li>Availability: member state changes, elections, primary absence.</li>
<li>Replication: lag in seconds versus the oplog window, oplog window hours.</li>
<li>Performance: operation latency percentiles by type, queued readers/writers, WiredTiger read and write tickets in use, slow-query rate.</li>
<li>Cache: dirty and used ratios, application-thread eviction (a strong signal of trouble).</li>
<li>Resources: disk utilization and latency, free space, CPU steal, memory, connections versus limits.</li>
<li>Correctness: backup success, last restore test, certificate expiry.</li></ul>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['profiler', 'slow queries'], q: 'How do you find the slowest queries on a cluster you have never seen?',
    a: `<p>Start with the logs: slow operations over <code>slowms</code> (100 ms default) are logged as structured JSON with plan summary, keys and docs examined. Group them by query shape (the <code>queryHash</code> / <code>planCacheShapeHash</code>), not by individual statement.</p>
<p>Then enable the profiler briefly at level 1 with a threshold or sample rate on the suspect database, use <code>$currentOp</code> for live long runners, and <code>$indexStats</code> to see which indexes are actually used. On Atlas, <code>$queryStats</code> gives aggregated per-shape metrics; on self-managed deployments, aggregate the slow-query log by <code>queryHash</code> or use PMM Query Analytics instead. Fix the top shapes by total time, not by single worst execution.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['indexes', 'operations'], q: 'How do index builds affect a production replica set?',
    a: `<p>Since 4.4 index builds are hybrid and run simultaneously on all data-bearing members: they take an exclusive lock only briefly at the start and end, and the primary commits once a quorum of data-bearing voting members has finished (<code>commitQuorum</code>, default <code>"votingMembers"</code>).</p>
<p>They still consume CPU, I/O and cache, and on a large collection can push replication lag and latency. Build during low traffic, watch cache and lag, and size <code>maxIndexBuildMemoryUsageMegabytes</code> appropriately. For extreme cases do a rolling build member by member in standalone mode.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['upgrades', 'fcv'], q: 'How do you upgrade a replica set from 6.0 to 7.0 with no downtime?',
    a: `<ol><li>Read the compatibility notes; check drivers support 7.0; take and verify a backup.</li>
<li>Confirm <code>featureCompatibilityVersion</code> is 6.0.</li>
<li>Upgrade secondaries one at a time (stop, replace binaries, start, wait for SECONDARY and caught up).</li>
<li><code>rs.stepDown()</code> the primary and upgrade it last.</li>
<li>Run in mixed binary / old FCV for a soak period, which keeps the downgrade path open.</li>
<li>Set FCV to 7.0 (<code>setFeatureCompatibilityVersion: "7.0", confirm: true</code>), which enables new on-disk features and closes the easy downgrade.</li></ol>
<p>Major versions must be upgraded one at a time (5.0 to 6.0 to 7.0). For sharded clusters: stop the balancer, upgrade config servers, then shards, then mongos.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['wiredtiger'], q: 'Explain the WiredTiger cache and what eviction pressure looks like.',
    a: `<p>WiredTiger keeps uncompressed pages in its own cache, by default 50% of (RAM minus 1 GB). The OS page cache holds compressed file blocks on top of that. Background eviction threads write dirty pages and evict clean ones to keep usage around 80% and dirty data around 5%.</p>
<p>When those targets are exceeded (95% used or 20% dirty), application threads are drafted into eviction, and latency spikes. Symptoms: high <code>pages evicted by application threads</code>, rising dirty ratio, read ticket exhaustion, and checkpoints taking longer. Fixes: reduce working set (indexes, projection), add RAM, faster disks, avoid huge transactions holding snapshots.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['wiredtiger', 'durability'], q: 'How does WiredTiger guarantee durability: journal versus checkpoints?',
    a: `<p>Writes go to the cache and to the write-ahead log (journal). The journal is flushed every 100 ms by default, immediately for <code>j: true</code> writes. Every 60 seconds a checkpoint writes a consistent snapshot of all data files.</p>
<p>On crash, the server opens the last checkpoint and replays the journal from that point. Without the journal you could lose up to 60 seconds of writes. In a replica set, majority-acknowledged writes are additionally safe because other members have them.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['storage'], q: 'Disk usage did not drop after deleting half the collection. Why, and what do you do?',
    a: `<p>WiredTiger reuses freed blocks within the file but does not return them to the operating system. The space shows as <code>file bytes available for reuse</code> in <code>db.coll.stats()</code>.</p>
<p>Options: let new writes reuse it; run <code>compact</code> one member at a time (it no longer blocks CRUD since 4.4, and 8.0 adds background <code>autoCompact</code>); or resync a secondary from scratch, which rebuilds compact files, then rotate it into primary. Also check whether indexes, not data, hold the space.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['connections'], q: 'The server reports thousands of connections. How do you approach it?',
    a: `<p>Each connection costs about 1 MB of memory and a thread, so connection storms degrade the server. Find the source with <code>$currentOp</code> / <code>db.serverStatus().connections</code> and client metadata (<code>appName</code>, host).</p>
<p>Typical causes: an app creating a new MongoClient per request instead of a singleton; very large <code>maxPoolSize</code> multiplied by many pods; serverless functions without pooling. Fix the client lifecycle, cap pool sizes so (pods x maxPoolSize) is within budget, set <code>maxIdleTimeMS</code>, and consider a connection proxy pattern for serverless.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['maintenance'], q: 'How do you do rolling maintenance (OS patching) on a replica set?',
    a: `<p>One member at a time: patch a secondary, restart, wait for SECONDARY state and zero lag; repeat for the other secondaries; then <code>rs.stepDown()</code> the primary, wait for a new primary, and patch the old one. Before starting, verify the oplog window comfortably exceeds the maintenance time and that applications use retryable writes and sane timeouts so the stepdown is a blip.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['initial sync'], q: 'A member needs a full resync of 3 TB. How do you make it safe and fast?',
    a: `<p>Logical initial sync copies all collections then rebuilds indexes, and must finish within the oplog window or it restarts. For multi-TB data prefer seeding from a snapshot: restore a recent filesystem or backup snapshot onto the member and let it catch up from the oplog. File-copy-based initial sync (Enterprise / Percona) achieves the same.</p>
<p>Either way: make sure the oplog window is larger than the copy time, pick the sync source deliberately (a secondary, not a busy primary), and watch the sync source's disk I/O.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['ftdc', 'diagnostics'], q: 'What is FTDC and when do you use it?',
    a: `<p>Full Time Diagnostic Data Capture: mongod and mongos write compressed samples of <code>serverStatus</code>, <code>replSetGetStatus</code>, system metrics and more, roughly once a second, into <code>diagnostic.data</code>. It is always on, with minimal overhead.</p>
<p>It is the black box recorder for incidents: when monitoring was too coarse, FTDC shows second-by-second tickets, cache, eviction, checkpoint and replication behaviour around the event. Ship the directory to support or decode it with tools such as keyhole or t2-style viewers.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['roles'], q: 'Design roles for an app, an analyst and a backup operator.',
    a: `<ul><li>App: custom role with <code>find/insert/update/remove</code> on its own database, no <code>dropCollection</code>, <code>dropDatabase</code> or index management, so a bug cannot destroy data.</li>
<li>Analyst: <code>read</code> on a reporting database or a view that excludes PII, with reads routed to an analytics-tagged hidden secondary.</li>
<li>Backup operator: built-in <code>backup</code> and <code>restore</code> roles, used only by the backup agent, credentials in a secrets manager.</li></ul>
<p>Separate human admins with <code>userAdminAnyDatabase</code> or <code>clusterAdmin</code> as needed, enforced through LDAP / OIDC groups, and audit privileged actions.</p>` }
  ]
},
{
  id: 'architect',
  title: 'Architect',
  desc: 'Sharding, shard keys, multi-region HA/DR, capacity planning, Kubernetes and migrations.',
  items: [
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['sharding'], q: 'What are the components of a sharded cluster?',
    a: `<p>Shards (each a replica set holding a subset of data), config servers (a replica set holding metadata: which chunks / ranges live where), and mongos routers (stateless query routers that the application connects to). Since 8.0 a config server can also be a shard (embedded config server).</p>
<p>A query that includes the shard key is targeted to the owning shard; otherwise mongos scatters it to all shards and merges results.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['sharding', 'shard key'], q: 'What makes a good shard key?',
    a: `<p>High cardinality (many distinct values so data can be split finely), low frequency (no single value dominating), non-monotonic writes (so inserts spread over shards instead of hitting the last chunk), and present in most queries (so reads are targeted).</p>
<p>Usually that means a compound key such as <code>{ tenantId: 1, orderId: 1 }</code>, or a hashed key when you only need even write distribution and do point lookups. A key that fails any of these creates jumbo chunks, hot shards or scatter-gather queries.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['sharding', 'hashed'], q: 'Hashed versus ranged sharding?',
    a: `<p>Hashed sharding distributes monotonically increasing keys (timestamps, ObjectIds) evenly and lets you pre-split; point lookups on the key are targeted but range queries on it become scatter-gather.</p>
<p>Ranged sharding keeps nearby values together, so range queries are efficient and you can use zones for data locality, but monotonic keys funnel all inserts to one shard. Compound hashed keys (for example <code>{ region: 1, userId: "hashed" }</code>) combine zone locality with even distribution.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['sharding', 'jumbo'], q: 'What is a jumbo chunk and how do you deal with it?',
    a: `<p>A chunk that exceeds the configured range size and cannot be split because all its documents share one shard-key value (or the key has too few values). The balancer cannot move it, so one shard grows unevenly.</p>
<p>Fix the cause, not the symptom: refine the shard key by adding a suffix field (<code>refineCollectionShardKey</code>, 4.4+) or reshard to a better key (<code>reshardCollection</code>, 5.0+). <code>clearJumboFlag</code> only helps when a split has become possible.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['sharding', 'resharding'], q: 'How does online resharding work and what does it need?',
    a: `<p><code>reshardCollection</code> creates a new collection partitioned by the new key on the recipient shards, copies the data, then tails the oplog to apply ongoing writes. When the recipients have caught up it blocks writes for a short critical section (a few seconds, bounded by <code>reshardingCriticalSectionTimeoutMillis</code>) and atomically switches.</p>
<p>Requirements: enough free storage (roughly 1.2x the collection on recipients), spare I/O and CPU, an oplog window that covers the copy, and applications that tolerate the brief write block. Recent releases (8.0) can also reshard to the same key to redistribute data evenly, for example after adding shards.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['zones'], q: 'How would you keep EU customer data in EU data centers in one cluster?',
    a: `<p>Shard on a key that starts with the region, such as <code>{ region: 1, customerId: 1 }</code>, place shards in each geography, and define zones: <code>sh.addShardToZone("shardEU", "EU")</code> and <code>sh.updateZoneKeyRange(ns, { region: "EU", customerId: MinKey }, { region: "EU", customerId: MaxKey }, "EU")</code>. The balancer keeps those ranges on EU shards.</p>
<p>Also place each shard's replica set members within its region, keep config servers where the compliance team accepts metadata, and make sure backups for EU shards stay in the EU.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['ha', 'dr'], q: 'Design a two-region deployment that survives a full region loss.',
    a: `<p>Two regions cannot form a majority on their own after one is lost, so use three failure domains: for example 2 members in region A, 2 in region B, and 1 in region C (or a third availability zone / small tie-breaker site). Any single region loss leaves 3 of 5 votes and an automatic election.</p>
<p>Use <code>w: "majority"</code> so acknowledged writes exist in at least two sites; set priorities so the primary prefers the region nearest the application; use tag sets for local reads. If a third site is impossible, accept a manual failover runbook (force reconfig on the survivors) and document the potential data loss.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['rpo', 'rto'], q: 'How do RPO and RTO map to MongoDB features?',
    a: `<p>RPO (data loss): <code>w: "majority"</code> across sites gives zero RPO for acknowledged writes in a failover; for logical corruption or human error, RPO depends on backup type: continuous oplog backup gives seconds, nightly dumps give up to 24 hours.</p>
<p>RTO (time to recover): automatic elections give seconds for node or site loss within a properly spread replica set; restoring from backup takes hours depending on data size, which is why delayed secondaries and PITR to a side cluster exist. State both numbers per failure type, not a single number.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['capacity'], q: 'How do you size RAM for a new MongoDB workload?',
    a: `<p>Estimate the working set: the indexes that are hit (usually all of them) plus the frequently accessed documents. Aim to keep that in the WiredTiger cache (about 50% of RAM minus 1 GB), leaving the rest for the OS page cache, connections and aggregation memory.</p>
<p>Inputs: document count and average size, growth per month, index sizes (prototype with real data and read <code>totalIndexSize</code>), compression ratio (snappy commonly 2-4x for JSON-like data), connection count. Then validate with a load test, watching cache hit ratio and application-thread eviction.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['capacity', 'sharding'], q: 'When do you shard, and when is it premature?',
    a: `<p>Shard when one replica set cannot meet the requirement even after tuning: working set exceeds the RAM you can buy, write throughput saturates one primary's disk, a single node's data size makes backups and resyncs unmanageable (multi-TB), or data residency requires geography.</p>
<p>It is premature when the problem is missing indexes, an inefficient schema or an undersized instance. Sharding adds operational complexity (config servers, balancer, shard-key permanence costs, scatter-gather). Vertical scaling and query fixes come first, but choose a shard-friendly key early if growth is certain.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['kubernetes'], q: 'What matters when you run MongoDB on Kubernetes?',
    a: `<ul><li>Use an operator (Percona, MongoDB Community / Enterprise operator) that understands replica-set membership, rolling restarts, backups and upgrades. A bare StatefulSet is not enough.</li>
<li>StatefulSets with stable network identities, PersistentVolumes on a fast storage class, <code>volumeBindingMode: WaitForFirstConsumer</code>, and anti-affinity across nodes and zones.</li>
<li>PodDisruptionBudgets so node drains never take more than one member, and resource requests equal to limits for predictable cache sizing (set the WiredTiger cache explicitly; containers can confuse RAM detection).</li>
<li>Backups to object storage with PITR, monitoring via exporters, and a tested restore into a fresh namespace.</li></ul>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['migration'], q: 'How would you migrate a 2 TB replica set to a new cluster with minimal downtime?',
    a: `<p>Choose a tool that does initial copy plus change data capture: <code>mongosync</code> (cluster-to-cluster sync, 6.0+), Percona's tooling, or an operator-based approach where you add new members to the existing replica set, let them sync, then remove the old ones (the simplest when topology allows).</p>
<p>Plan: verify version compatibility; run the copy; monitor lag until it is steady; verify counts and checksums (<code>dbHash</code> per collection); freeze writes or switch the application connection string during a short window; keep the source writable-but-unused for rollback. Rehearse once on a copy.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['driver'], q: 'What connection string options do you always set?',
    a: `<p>A seed list with several members (or <code>mongodb+srv://</code>), <code>replicaSet</code>, <code>retryWrites=true</code> (default) and <code>retryReads=true</code>, an explicit <code>w=majority</code>, sane <code>maxPoolSize</code> per process, <code>connectTimeoutMS</code> and <code>serverSelectionTimeoutMS</code> appropriate for failover (a few seconds to 30 s), <code>appName</code> for diagnostics, and TLS options. Per operation, use <code>maxTimeMS</code> or client-side timeouts (CSOT, <code>timeoutMS</code>) so queries cannot hang forever.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['read preference'], q: 'When is reading from secondaries a good idea?',
    a: `<p>Good: analytics and reporting on a dedicated (often hidden or tagged) secondary, geographically local reads where staleness is acceptable, and keeping heavy batch jobs off the primary.</p>
<p>Bad: scaling general read throughput (every secondary already does the full write workload, so headroom is limited), reads that must see the user's latest write (unless you use causal consistency), and anything where a failover would suddenly double load on the remaining nodes. <code>maxStalenessSeconds</code> bounds staleness when you do use secondaries.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['multi-tenant'], q: 'Database per tenant, collection per tenant, or shared collection?',
    a: `<p>A shared collection with <code>tenantId</code> leading every index (and the shard key) scales to many tenants with the fewest files and lowest overhead. Collection or database per tenant gives isolation and easy deletion but each collection and index is a WiredTiger file: tens of thousands of them slow startup, checkpoints and backups.</p>
<p>A common hybrid: shared collections for small tenants, dedicated databases or clusters for very large or regulated tenants, routed by a tenant directory.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['time series'], q: 'How do you design for IoT telemetry at 100k inserts per second?',
    a: `<p>Use a time-series collection with <code>timeField</code>, a <code>metaField</code> for the device identity and an appropriate granularity (or bucket span settings); it applies the bucket pattern and columnar compression internally. Write in batches with unordered inserts and <code>w: "majority"</code> or <code>w: 1</code> depending on loss tolerance.</p>
<p>At this rate, shard on the metaField (hashed or ranged by device) to spread writes, set TTL (<code>expireAfterSeconds</code>) for raw retention, and roll up into hourly summaries with scheduled aggregations using <code>$merge</code>. Size disks for the write rate and keep secondary indexes minimal.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['ops manager'], q: 'Ops Manager, Percona tooling or roll your own: how do you choose for self-managed MongoDB?',
    a: `<p>Ops Manager (Enterprise licence) gives automation, monitoring and continuous backup in one product with vendor support. Percona Server for MongoDB plus PBM and PMM is open source with enterprise features (encryption, LDAP, audit) and PITR. Rolling your own means scripts, mongodump or snapshots, and Prometheus exporters.</p>
<p>Criteria: licence budget and support needs, Kubernetes strategy (operators), required backup RPO, encryption and audit compliance, and team skills. The worst outcome is a homegrown mix with no tested restore.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['cost'], q: 'How do you reduce the cost of a large MongoDB estate without losing reliability?',
    a: `<ul><li>Delete or archive cold data (TTL, online archive to object storage, rolled-up summaries).</li>
<li>Drop unused indexes (<code>$indexStats</code> over a full business cycle) and redundant prefixes; hide first, drop later.</li>
<li>Right-size instances from real cache and CPU metrics; compress with zstd where CPU allows.</li>
<li>Consolidate tiny replica sets onto shared clusters with resource isolation where acceptable.</li>
<li>Fix the top query shapes, which often allows a smaller tier.</li></ul>` }
  ]
},
{
  id: 'scenario',
  title: 'Scenarios / SRE',
  desc: 'Incident-style questions: what you check first, why, and how you fix the root cause.',
  items: [
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['incident', 'latency'], q: 'Latency on the main API jumped from 20 ms to 2 s at 10:00. What do you do?',
    a: `<ol><li>Scope: all operations or one shape? One database or all? Correlate with deploys, batch jobs and traffic.</li>
<li>Live view: <code>db.currentOp({ secs_running: { $gte: 1 } })</code> for long runners and waiting locks; check queued operations and ticket availability.</li>
<li>Logs: slow-query lines since 10:00 grouped by query shape and plan summary; a new COLLSCAN or a plan flip is the most common cause.</li>
<li>Resources: cache dirty ratio and application-thread eviction, disk latency, CPU, replication lag.</li></ol>
<p>Mitigate first (kill the runaway operation, roll back the deploy, add the missing index on a hidden basis, set a plan cache filter if a plan flipped), then fix the root cause and add an alert for that signal.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['incident', 'disk'], q: 'Disk is 92% full on the primary. What is your plan?',
    a: `<p>Immediate: confirm what is growing (data files, indexes, journal, logs, <code>diagnostic.data</code>, oplog) with <code>db.stats()</code> per database and the filesystem. Rotate and compress logs. If the volume can be expanded online (cloud / CSI), expand it now.</p>
<p>Short term: drop unused indexes, archive or TTL old data, then reclaim with <code>compact</code> or a rolling resync. Do not delete files under the dbPath by hand. Longer term: alert at 70 and 80%, and forecast growth from the weekly trend.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['incident', 'election'], q: 'The application logs NotWritablePrimary errors every few minutes. Diagnose.',
    a: `<p>Repeated stepdowns or elections. Check <code>rs.status()</code> and logs for election reasons: heartbeat timeouts (network flaps, overloaded primary, long GC-like stalls from swap or disk), priority takeover (a higher-priority member catching up then taking over), or someone running <code>stepDown</code>.</p>
<p>Look at the term number increasing over time, member health and pings. Fix the infrastructure cause; meanwhile make sure the driver uses retryable writes and a reasonable <code>serverSelectionTimeoutMS</code>, so a single election is invisible to users.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['incident', 'rollback'], q: 'After a failover, some orders are missing. What happened and how do you recover them?',
    a: `<p>Most likely the old primary accepted writes with <code>w: 1</code> that never reached a majority; when it rejoined, those writes were rolled back. They are saved as BSON in the <code>rollback/</code> directory under the dbPath (one file per namespace).</p>
<p>Recover: inspect the files with <code>bsondump</code>, reconcile against current data (some may have been re-created by the application), and re-insert the missing ones. Prevent: use <code>w: "majority"</code> for business-critical writes, which by definition cannot be rolled back.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['incident', 'cache'], q: 'CPU is fine but latency is spiky and the cache is 20% dirty. What is going on?',
    a: `<p>Dirty cache at the 20% trigger means application threads are being forced to help with eviction, which shows up as latency spikes rather than CPU saturation. Typical causes: write bursts faster than the disk can absorb, very large transactions or long-running snapshots pinning old versions, or slow checkpoints on a degraded volume.</p>
<p>Check disk write latency and throughput, checkpoint duration, long-running transactions in <code>currentOp</code>, and the write pattern (bulk loads without throttling). Fix the disk or throttle the bulk jobs; only tune eviction parameters after the root cause is understood.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['incident', 'index'], q: 'A query was fast yesterday and is slow today with the same data. Why?',
    a: `<p>Most often a plan change: the plan cache entry was discarded (restart, index created or dropped, or replanning because the cached plan did far more work than expected) and the planner picked a different index from a trial run on non-representative values. <code>explain("allPlansExecution")</code> shows the candidates; the slow-query log line shows <code>planSummary</code> and <code>replanned</code>.</p>
<p>Fixes: create an index that makes the right plan obviously best (usually an ESR-ordered compound index), remove the misleading index, or, as a temporary pin, set an index filter / <code>planCacheSetFilter</code> or a <code>hint()</code> in the application.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['incident', 'sharding'], q: 'One shard is at 90% CPU while the others are idle. What do you investigate?',
    a: `<p>Hot shard. Check write distribution: a monotonic shard key (timestamps, ObjectId) sends every insert to the max chunk. Check read targeting: a popular tenant or key value concentrated on one shard. Check chunk distribution and jumbo chunks with <code>sh.status()</code> / <code>$shardedDataDistribution</code>, and balancer health.</p>
<p>Short term: move hot ranges, add zones or split chunks. Long term: refine or reshard the key (add a high-cardinality suffix, or use a hashed component).</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['incident', 'oplog'], q: 'A secondary went into RECOVERING and reports "too stale to catch up". What now?',
    a: `<p>Its last applied operation has rolled off the sync sources' oplogs, so it can never catch up incrementally. It must be resynced: wipe its dbPath and let initial sync run, or seed it from a recent snapshot and let it catch up.</p>
<p>Before resyncing, make sure the oplog is now large enough to cover the resync time (resize with <code>replSetResizeOplog</code>), otherwise the resync will fail the same way. Then find out why it fell behind (outage length, disk performance) and alert on lag versus oplog window.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['incident', 'connections'], q: 'After a deploy, connections jumped from 800 to 15,000 and the primary is struggling.',
    a: `<p>Identify the client with <code>db.currentOp(true)</code> grouped by <code>clientMetadata.application.name</code> and source host. The usual cause is a code change creating a MongoClient per request or per call, or a pool size multiplied by a large replica count.</p>
<p>Mitigate by rolling back the deploy or scaling down that service; the server cannot fix a client leak. Then fix the client lifecycle, add a connection count alert, and consider <code>net.maxIncomingConnections</code> as a safety ceiling.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['incident', 'transactions'], q: 'The app reports many TransientTransactionError and WriteConflict errors. How do you fix it?',
    a: `<p>Transactions are contending on the same documents (a hot counter, a shared inventory document) or are long-running, so concurrent transactions conflict and abort. Retrying is expected, but high rates waste work and add latency.</p>
<p>Shorten transactions (no external calls inside them), touch hot documents last, split hot documents (sharded counters, bucketed inventory), or avoid the transaction by using a single-document atomic update with a conditional filter such as <code>{ _id, stock: { $gte: qty } }</code> plus <code>$inc</code>.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['incident', 'ttl'], q: 'A TTL index is not deleting expired documents. Why?',
    a: `<p>Common causes: the field is a string or number, not a BSON Date (TTL ignores non-dates); the index is compound (TTL needs a single-field index); the TTL monitor runs every 60 seconds and deletes in batches, so it can lag under heavy load; or the node is a secondary (deletes happen on the primary and replicate).</p>
<p>Check with <code>db.coll.getIndexes()</code> and a sample document's field type, and <code>serverStatus().metrics.ttl</code> for passes and deleted documents.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['incident', 'security'], q: 'You discover a production database exposed to the internet without auth. Steps?',
    a: `<ol><li>Contain: firewall or security group to private networks immediately; do not shut down if that loses evidence you need.</li>
<li>Assess: check logs and the audit trail for unknown client IPs, look for ransom notes or dropped databases, compare counts with the last backup.</li>
<li>Remediate: enable auth and TLS, rotate any credentials that could have been read, bind to private IPs.</li>
<li>Recover data from backups if tampered, and notify security and compliance per policy (this may be a reportable breach).</li>
<li>Prevent: infrastructure policy that blocks public exposure, configuration scanning, and alerting on auth-disabled instances.</li></ol>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['incident', 'index build'], q: 'An index build on a 1 TB collection is causing replication lag. What do you do?',
    a: `<p>Decide whether to continue: <code>currentOp</code> shows build progress. If lag is approaching the oplog window, abort it with <code>db.coll.dropIndex(name)</code> (aborts an in-progress build) or <code>killOp</code>.</p>
<p>Then rebuild in a low-traffic window, or do a rolling build: take each secondary out as a standalone on another port, build the index, rejoin, catch up, then step down and do the primary. Also check whether a smaller partial index would satisfy the query.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['incident', 'balancer'], q: 'Chunks are not balancing across the new shard you added. What do you check?',
    a: `<p><code>sh.getBalancerState()</code> and <code>sh.isBalancerRunning()</code>; the balancer window settings; whether collections are actually sharded; whether chunks are jumbo; whether zones restrict ranges away from the new shard; and migration errors in the config server logs (<code>config.changelog</code>).</p>
<p>Since 6.0 the balancer works on data size differences per collection, not chunk counts, so a small difference may be considered balanced. Disk space on the new shard and network throughput also limit migrations.</p>` },
  { level: 'senior', tags: ['incident', 'rca'], q: 'How do you write a good RCA for a database incident?',
    a: `<p>Timeline with UTC timestamps from detection to resolution; customer impact in numbers; the trigger and the root cause (separately: the trigger is what happened, the root cause is why the system allowed it); contributing factors; what went well and what slowed us down; and action items with owners and dates, each mapped to a failure it prevents.</p>
<p>Keep it blameless and evidence-based (logs, FTDC graphs, explain output), and track action items to closure; repeat incidents usually mean old action items were not done.</p>` },
  { level: 'mid', tags: ['incident', 'memory'], q: 'mongod was killed by the OOM killer. What do you look at?',
    a: `<p>Check whether the WiredTiger cache size is appropriate for the container or host (in containers it must be set explicitly or derived from the cgroup limit), how many connections were open (about 1 MB each), large in-memory aggregations or sorts, and other processes on the host competing for RAM.</p>
<p>Also check swap configuration and <code>vm.overcommit</code>. Set memory limits and the cache size consistently, cap connections, and add an alert on resident memory.</p>` }
  ]
}
];
